This is a toy problem with a simple ascending flank, one
reflection, and a truth case data set which generates a chunk of
brine, then oil, then gas, as we go updip. The reflection brightens
as we go into hydrocarbons. The prior is 1/3:1/3:1/3 for the three fluids
(set in the default XML; varies_areally is switched *off* so the model
doesn't read the varying probabilities which we used to generate the
model in the make_flank_model.pl script. When the truth case was
generated, varies_areally is switched on for the reservoir layer.

This problem sketches the kind of framework that might be useful for people
who do hydrocarbon probability studies from a single reflection amplitude. 
This model is probably OK for very thick reservoirs where the seal/reservoir 
event is picked: obviously  no tuning effects are taken into account. Near 
and far stack information is also used. The fluid detection is only marginally 
better with the far stack added in.


To draw samples, e.g.

% delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 -RWS -N 1000

To see the gas probability...(gas=3 in layer=2)

% deliveryAnalyser -i realisations.su --fluid-prob 3 2

To see the oil probability...(oil=1 in layer=2)

% deliveryAnalyser -i realisations.su --fluid-prob 1 2

To see MAP model wiggles & fluid type...
% delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 --MAP
% deliveryAnalyser -i realisation.MAP.su -S 1.8 2 R_near wavelet.su near_stack_synth.su -lf -G


P.S.
To make the synthetic, one typically does something like

% delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 --make-synthetic 40 4 1.6 2.1

then copy the synthetic output seismics to the desired files.
